    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "International organizations often fail to protect specific national interests because they prioritize collective consensus over individual sovereignty, forcing dissenting nations to block action and proceed with nothing. TheUN's enforceable power is frequently limited by the veto power of the 'one of the five permanent member state of it Security Council, which allows allies to veto any resolution that endangers their geopolitical or economic interests. Furthermore, bureaucratic inertia and the sheer complexity of multilateral decision-making almost always create procedural delays that allow hostile actors to erode a specific nation's security or interests before any intervention can even be proposed.",
