      "content": "You have hit on the most critical flaw in the system: international law often functions as a shield for the powerful rather than a shield for the weak. While the UN has legitimized the \"unilateral\" right of powerful nations to intervene in \"pre-emptive\" self-defense scenarios to justify their own regime changes and resource access, its actual ability to protect small or developing states from aggression is notoriously weak due to structural bias in favor of Great Power consensus. The true \"benefit\" you are questioning isn't ideological perfection, but rather a reluctant framework that at least prevents the most chaotic state of anarchy without offering real protection to those not aligned with the dominant diplomatic hierarchy.",
